g **T5**: Part of a completed [T5,T6,T7] meld by opponent. They\u2019ve already used it \u2014 probably safe. Also, I held T5 without using it in a chow \u2014 suggests limited value.\n- Playing **T7**: Used in [T5,T6,T7] \u2014 opponent has already melded it. Also, I have T7 but didn\u2019t complete a chow with it \u2014 implies others may see it as low risk. Likely safe.\n- Playing **T1**: Already used in my own meld [T1,T2,T3], and T1s have been played before. Low demand expected.\n\nReason: Safety is paramount. Among options, **T3**, **T2**, **T1** are all parts of already-exposed chows ([T1,T2,T3]) \u2014 making them safer to discard. However, **T3** was just drawn or held \u2014 better to remove earlier-settled tiles. **T7** stands out: it was part of an opponent's melded [T5,T6,T7], so they clearly had T7 and didn't protect it. Similarly, **T5** is from same set. But **T7** has lower connection to my hand structure and hasn't been involved in any chi near me. Moreover, T8 is extinct (0 left), suggesting strong interest in 7-8-9 sequences \u2014 but since [T5,T6,T7] is already melded, T7 is likely safe. Between T1, T2, T3, and T7 \u2014 **T7** offers least strategic value to opponents while being disconnected from current draws.\n\nAnswer: Play T7"}
